    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Personal history of treated brucellosis (Z86.19). No evidence of relapse or a focal complication. Relapses usually come within months of treatment and present with fever, which she has not had; she has no spinal or sacroiliac findings, no murmur, no organ enlargement, and normal inflammatory markers and blood count. [S]</item><item>Repeat testing. Brucella agglutinating antibodies can stay positive for a long time after successful treatment, so a repeat titer cannot separate past from active infection and would only cause confusion. Blood cultures are useful only if fever returns. [S]</item><item>Other infections. Nothing in her exposures calls for further infection testing now. The one-time HIV, hepatitis B, and hepatitis C screening recommended for all adults is reasonable to complete with her preventive care. [S]</item><item>Post-infectious ME/CFS (G93.32). Her course fits a post-infectious fatigue syndrome after brucellosis rather than ongoing infection. Prolonged or repeated antibiotics do not help post-infectious fatigue and carry real harms. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: The intake exclusion tests reviewed this morning: normal blood count, metabolic panel, liver tests, ESR 8 mm/h, CRP 1.2 mg/L, creatine kinase, and morning cortisol; 25-hydroxyvitamin D low at 17 ng/mL. The intake standing test. The report of the positive Brucella titer and the treatment record from abroad. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

